## 11. Accessibility and Readability

- **Color is never the only signal.** Each player has a color AND a pattern AND an initial on the board and in profiles.
- **Contrast:** body text must be at least 4.5:1 against its background. Large display text at least 3:1.
- **Text sizes:** body text never below 15 sp. Score numbers always at display.m or larger.
- **Outlines** remain on all interactive elements so they stay visible without relying on color alone.
- **Reduced motion:** disables bounce, line draw-in, stamp pop, and teddy animations. Static poses remain.
- **Hit targets:** minimum 48 px for buttons; board lines use an invisible 30 px hit area.
- **Dyslexia-friendly option (setting):** switches body text to a more open face such as Lexend and increases line spacing to 1.6.
- **Sound:** every sound effect has a visual equivalent. Sounds are optional and default on, with a single toggle in Settings.

---

## 12. Motion and Sound Feel

### 12.1 Motion
- Bouncy but short: spring easing for pops and stamps (duration 200–280 ms).
- Teddy bounce: 300 ms, once per trigger.
- Screen transitions: slide and fade in 280 ms; paper "flip" transition for Results.
- No looping animations except the speaking ring in Voice (honey pulse, 1.2 s cycle, paused when muted).
- No particle confetti. Use three-star bursts or a single paper ribbon wave on wins.

### 12.2 Sound (prototype spec)
- Line draw: soft pencil scratch, 120 ms.
- Box claim: a bright wooden "tock" with a small chime.
- Turn change: a soft wooden knock.
- Level complete: a short fanfare (two bars, marimba or xylophone timbre).
- Match win: a longer celebratory jingle.
- Chat message: soft paper rustle.
- Level up: a rising three-note chime.

Sound files are placeholders in the prototype. Note these as requirements in the build, not as finished audio.

